is the first few chapters,

00:08:26.603 --> 00:08:28.626
is preparing for the journey, right?

00:08:28.626 --> 00:08:29.779
Getting ready to go on this journey.

00:08:29.779 --> 00:08:32.626
The first part of the book
is all about the mental stuff

00:08:32.626 --> 00:08:34.025
that you need to clear out,

00:08:34.025 --> 00:08:35.293
before you can start the business,

00:08:35.293 --> 00:08:37.714
because, again, just a simple
example of the barriers

00:08:37.714 --> 00:08:41.093
about a business being very risky,

00:08:41.093 --> 00:08:43.942
or another one we get a lot
is, "I don't have any ideas.

00:08:43.942 --> 00:08:46.261
"I can't start a business,
unless I have an idea."

00:08:46.261 --> 00:08:47.681
The first few chapters of the book

00:08:47.681 --> 00:08:50.130
deal with how to overcome
these really common,

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:52.461
but very pernicious mental barriers,

00:08:52.461 --> 00:08:53.294
because we know

00:08:53.294 --> 00:08:55.532
that a lot of the games
we play with ourselves

00:08:55.532 --> 00:08:57.323
are like weird, defense mechanisms,

00:08:57.323 --> 00:09:00.690
to get us to not, to not fail, right?

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:02.031
But, we need to be able

00:09:02.031 --> 00:09:04.530
to surpass those steps of limitations.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:06.277
I mean, look at all the people
on your wall behind you.

00:09:06.277 --> 00:09:07.424
You know, you got Jobs.

00:09:07.424 --> 00:09:08.979
I know you have Kanye
back there somewhere.

00:09:08.979 --> 00:09:09.812
All of these guys are people

00:09:09.812 --> 00:09:12.111
who were able to master their mindset,

00:09:12.111 --> 00:09:15.100
and push past the fear complex.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:16.598
And so, we spend a lot of time

00:09:16.598 --> 00:09:19.088
talking about how to overcome these fears,

00:09:19.088 --> 00:09:21.677
and we also go through a
lot of different scenarios,

00:09:21.677 --> 00:09:24.687
to try to completely
make some of these fears

00:09:24.687 --> 00:09:25.680
seem irrational.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:26.571
So, we go through different exercises

00:09:26.571 --> 00:09:27.880
to make those seem irrational,

00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:30.170
and how to stop
procrastinating with yourself.

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:31.482
So, that's the first thing.

00:09:31.482 --> 00:09:33.340
Second thing, second phase,

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:35.673
which is like the middle chapters
of the book we move into,

00:09:35.673 --> 00:09:36.622
strategy and tactics.

00:09:36.622 --> 00:09:39.522
So, what are the specific
ways to start the business?

00:09:39.522 --> 00:09:40.654
What's my philosophy behind it?

00:09:40.654 --> 00:09:42.122
So, we go through the freelancing thing.

00:09:42.122 --> 00:09:43.158
We go through how to scale up,

00:09:43.158 --> 00:09:45.869
from zero, to six figures,

00:09:45.869 --> 00:09:47.259
just with a service-based business.

00:09:47.259 --> 00:09:48.397
And then, we also talk about

00:09:48.397 --> 00:09:50.525
how to move into online world.

00:09:50.525 --> 00:09:53.148
So, how to go from, I'm just
performing this whole service.

00:09:53.148 --> 00:09:53.981
Hey, it's Daniel!

00:09:53.981 --> 00:09:55.699
I'm here to, like, personal train you!

00:09:55.699 --> 00:09:56.718
To, hey, it's Daniel.

00:09:56.718 --> 00:09:59.897
Now, I'm online, and I
have a company, right?

00:09:59.897 --> 00:10:00.845
Two different things.

00:10:00.845 --> 00:10:02.236
We talk about that transition.

00:10:02.236 --> 00:10:04.340
And the third phase of the book is like,

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:05.173
kind of the aftermath,

00:10:05.173 --> 00:10:06.566
and this is a lot of the
stuff I had to learn, too,

00:10:06.566 --> 00:10:07.416
along the way.

00:10:07.416 --> 00:10:09.132
Okay, you're makin' a
little bit of money, now.

00:10:09.132 --> 00:10:11.872
How do you deal with the,
with some of the weird things

00:10:11.872 --> 00:10:12.734
that come up as an entrepreneur.

00:10:12.734 --> 00:10:14.252
How do you stay focused?

00:10:14.252 --> 00:10:16.435
How do you, how do you
adjust your mindset,

00:10:16.435 --> 00:10:18.463
to the fact that you're makin'
a little bit more money now,

00:10:18.463 --> 00:10:20.843
but you have to really be your own boss.

00:10:20.843 --> 00:10:21.685
What does that mean?

00:10:21.685 --> 00:10:23.145
And so, the third
section talks about that,

00:10:23.145 --> 00:10:25.264
and it kind of wraps it up.

00:10:25.264 --> 00:10:26.097
- [Evan] I like it.

00:10:26.097 --> 00:10:26.930
Cool.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:28.968
Well, let's get to some questions
here, from the live chat.

00:10:28.968 --> 00:10:30.834
You guys have questions for Daniel.

00:10:30.834 --> 00:10:31.667
Leave it there.

00:10:31.667 --> 00:10:34.607
I know we already got a
ton of people chiming in.

00:10:34.607 --> 00:10:36.235
BestUPro.

00:10:36.235 --> 00:10:37.329
"Are you familiar with the concept

00:10:37.329 --> 00:10:40.579
"of pulling from both sides, by Gary V?

00:10:41.587 --> 00:10:42.531
"And what are your thoughts?"

00:10:42.531 --> 00:10:44.470
Are you familiar with that,
pulling from both sides?

00:10:44.470 --> 00:10:45.303
- I'm not.

00:10:45.303 --> 00:10:46.260
Do you know who that is, Evan?

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:48.219
- [Evan] I'm not super familiar.

00:10:48.219 --> 00:10:51.641
I want to say it's his
clouds and dirt idea,

00:10:51.641 --> 00:10:54.741
where it's, the clouds
is like the strategy,

00:10:54.741 --> 00:10:57.452
and the big picture stuff, but
then, also, doing the work.

00:10:57.452 --> 00:10:58.443
- Right.

00:10:58.443 --> 00:10:59.276
- Hustling everyday.

00:10:59.276 --> 00:11:02.566
But, I don't want to say
that that's necessarily

00:11:02.566 --> 00:11:03.399
pulling from both sides.

00:11:03.399 --> 00:11:04.342
- [Daniel] It sounds good.

00:11:04.342 --> 00:11:05.175
- [Evan] Anyway, okay.

00:11:05.175 --> 00:11:06.951
If you you give us some more
clarity on that, BestUPro,

00:11:06.951 --> 00:11:10.280
then maybe we can answer it for you.

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:11.261
Next.

00:11:11.261 --> 00:11:12.094
Pervina.

00:11:12.094 --> 00:11:14.718
"How much time should we
spend assessing the demands?"

00:11:14.718 --> 00:11:16.106
So, back to what you were
talking, with the beginning,

00:11:16.106 --> 00:11:17.607
assess the demand.

00:11:17.607 --> 00:11:18.605
How do people go about it?

00:11:18.605 --> 00:11:19.734
You were talking about Upwork.

00:11:19.734 --> 00:11:22.424
And then, how much time should
they spend on that part?

00:11:22.424 --> 00:11:23.908
- [Daniel] Yeah, so just to be clear,

00:11:23.908 --> 00:11:27.146
this is a very good
example for an interview,

00:11:27.146 --> 00:11:30.168
because it's a very clear
example of how to find ideas.

00:11:30.168 --> 00:11:31.001
But, at the same time,

00:11:31.001 --> 00:11:34.629
Upwork is a super simple,
but only one example,

00:11:34.629 --> 00:11:36.991
of how to find work.

00:11:36.991 --> 00:11:39.209
In terms of how to assess the demand,

00:11:39.209 --> 00:11:41.441
I think a few things
have to come into play.

00:11:41.441 --> 00:11:43.101
First of all, just know that the process

00:11:43.101 --> 00:11:45.829
of you looking out in the
world, and asking yourself,

00:11:45.829 --> 00:11:47.449
is this a liable business model?

00:11:47.449 --> 00:11:48.415
It is part of that process.

00:11:48.415 --> 00:11:50.712
And if you go through this process,

00:11:50.712 --> 00:11:52.905
another way would be to
look at Yelp reviews,

00:11:52.905 --> 00:11:53.976
or to go on Amazon,

00:11:53.976 --> 00:11:55.140
and see what people are complaining about,

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:56.897
and see where there are paying points.

00:11:56.897 --> 00:11:58.121
As you go through this process,

00:11:58.121 --> 00:11:59.820
you're going to become better at assessing

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:04.608
what's real, what's a real
need, versus what's a...

00:12:04.608 --> 00:12:05.441
Noise.

00:12:05.441 --> 00:12:06.843
What's just static.

00:12:06.843 --> 00:12:09.260
And, you might go
through a few iterations,

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.225
of a couple different business ideas,

00:12:11.225 --> 00:12:12.099
that you think are good,

00:12:12.099 --> 00:12:13.469
and they seem to have
some sort of relevance.

00:12:13.469 --> 00:12:14.447
And then, you start testing them,

00:12:14.447 --> 00:12:15.945
and no one really wants that.

00:12:15.945 --> 00:12:17.486
And that would be okay.

00:12:17.486 --> 00:12:19.625
It would be okay, if you
didn't hit a home run,

00:12:19.625 --> 00:12:21.408
out of the park, the first time,

00:12:21.408 --> 00:12:23.493
because as an entrepreneur,

00:12:23.493 --> 00:12:25.200
a lot of this comes down to testing.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:26.521
And so, you're building the skillset

00:12:26.521 --> 00:12:29.281
of identifying a good idea.

00:12:29.281 --> 00:12:30.370
And that's why, a lot of times,

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:32.959
when you see really sharp entrepreneurs,

00:12:32.959 --> 00:12:34.819
men and women who get good at business,

00:12:34.819 --> 00:12:36.709
they can instantly assess an idea.

00:12:36.709 --> 00:12:39.249
One of my friends, Lori
Greiner, from Shark Tank,

00:12:39.249 --> 00:12:41.437
has this whole concept,
called hero or zero.

00:12:41.437 --> 00:12:42.857
And she can instantly tell,

00:12:42.857 --> 00:12:44.009
I don't know how accurate she is,

00:12:44.009 --> 00:12:45.192
but she seems to be pretty accurate.

00:12:45.192 --> 00:12:46.429
She's doing well on the show.

00:12:46.429 --> 00:12:47.679
She can instantly tell
whether their business idea's

00:12:47.679 --> 00:12:48.779
going to be good, or not.

00:12:48.779 --> 00:12:50.272
And I think that comes
with time, and experience.

00:12:50.272 --> 00:12:53.165
So, it's okay if you spend
a little bit of a time,

00:12:53.165 --> 00:12:53.998
and it flops,

00:12:53.998 --> 00:12:55.423
or a lot of bit of time, and it does well.

00:12:55.423 --> 00:12:58.584
Just find an idea, and don't
wait too long to iterate,

00:12:58.584 --> 00:13:00.093
because what really stops you,

00:13:00.093 --> 00:13:03.524
is the paralysis by analysis, you know?

00:13:03.524 --> 00:13:05.953
Spend a few days looking it up.

00:13:05.953 --> 00:13:07.413
See if it's a good fit.

00:13:07.413 --> 00:13:09.784
And then, start planting out some seeds.

00:13:09.784 --> 00:13:10.617
- [Evan] Cool.

00:13:10.617 --> 00:13:12.063
So, when we were talkin' about,

00:13:12.063 --> 00:13:12.896
before you were sayin',

00:13:12.896 --> 00:13:15.462
makin' that transition
from a full-time job,

00:13:15.462 --> 00:13:17.801
to your side hustle making
that your full-time thing.

00:13:17.801 --> 00:13:19.560
And what was the percentage
you gave it, 60 percent,

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:20.393
70 percent?

00:13:20.393 --> 00:13:21.623
- I say it's 70, yeah.

00:13:21.623 --> 00:13:22.915
- [Evan] So, when you're
making 60, to 70 percent

00:13:22.915 --> 00:13:25.063
of your income, from your side hustle,

00:13:25.063 --> 00:13:27.918
then you quit, and you go
full-in on your business...

00:13:27.918 --> 00:13:31.550
You got a question comin'
in from Valley, saying,

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:32.859
and this is great time management,

00:13:32.859 --> 00:13:34.948
"How can I start a business,

00:13:34.948 --> 00:13:39.003
"if I work 12 hours at
my job, already, a day?"

00:13:39.003 --> 00:13:39.967
- [Daniel] I mean, okay.

00:13:39.967 --> 00:13:41.708
So, there's multiple, different
ways to think about this.

00:13:41.708 --> 00:13:45.467
The first way, is just like
the very, in your face,

00:13:45.467 --> 00:13:48.416
Gary V-type of like,
it's the second shift.

00:13:48.416 --> 00:13:50.634
It's the other seven-to-two,

00:13:50.634 --> 00:13:52.035
where it's like, you get home,

00:13:52.035 --> 00:13:53.018
you do your thing with your family,

00:13:53.018 --> 00:13:53.852
and you go back to work.

00:13:53.852 --> 00:13:54.801
There is that.

00:13:54.801 --> 00:13:57.028
I mean, that always is an option.

00:13:57.028 --> 00:13:59.718
But, I think that can burn
you out, after a while.

00:13:59.718 --> 00:14:01.918
So, you have to really be able to assess

00:14:01.918 --> 00:14:04.210
how important it is, the business, to you?

00:14:04.210 --> 00:14:07.401
It is a valid, working 12
hours a day, is a valid thing,

00:14:07.401 --> 00:14:08.423
and you do have to sleep.

00:14:08.423 --> 00:14:09.256
I get it.

00:14:09.256 --> 00:14:10.762
But, at the end of the day, you know,

00:14:10.762 --> 00:14:14.463
if you can put in, at least,
one-to-two hours per day,

00:14:14.463 --> 00:14:15.824
and I would actually say,

00:14:15.824 --> 00:14:18.372
try to find one block,
where you can do that.

00:14:18.372 --> 00:14:20.413
Four 30-minute blocks
is not the same thing

00:14:20.413 --> 00:14:22.041
as one two-hour block.

00:14:22.041 --> 00:14:25.379
So, if you could find,
even a one-hour block,

00:14:25.379 --> 00:14:27.074
to get some good work done,

00:14:27.074 --> 00:14:29.114
once a day is still seven hours a week,

00:14:29.114 --> 00:14:30.214
especially on the weekends, too.

00:14:30.214 --> 00:14:31.047
Remember the weekends,

00:14:31.047 --> 00:14:32.075
you should be be able to work with 'em,

00:14:32.075 --> 00:14:32.908
an hour on the weekends.

00:14:32.908 --> 00:14:36.159
And remember, that if you can
go into that one-hour block,

00:14:36.159 --> 00:14:37.688
with a plan of what you're going to do,

00:14:37.688 --> 00:14:39.128
the hour will be more effective.

00:14:39.128 --> 00:14:41.006
A lot of times, it takes
us 30-to-40 minutes,

00:14:41.006 --> 00:14:43.978
just to like clarify what we need to do.

00:14:43.978 --> 00:14:45.519
So, don't go into the block of time,

00:14:45.519 --> 00:14:46.465
if you only have a little bit of time,

00:14:46.465 --> 00:14:48.338
don't go into the time with no plan.

00:14:48.338 --> 00:14:49.966
Spend some time, during your work day.

00:14:49.966 --> 00:14:52.035
I'm sure you have 15 minutes
during your work day,

00:14:52.035 --> 00:14:53.156
to plot out what you're going to do

00:14:53.156 --> 00:14:54.616
when you get to your power hour,

00:14:54.616 --> 00:14:55.666
or whatever you want to call it,

00:14:55.666 --> 00:14:57.466
and go in there, crunch for an hour,

00:14:57.466 --> 00:14:59.486
and I think seven-to-ten hours a week

00:14:59.486 --> 00:15:00.554
is not hard to squeeze out.

00:15:00.554 --> 00:15:03.355
If you really peek at it,
that's 40, 40 hours a month.

00:15:03.355 --> 00:15:05.335
You can do something with that.

00:15:05.335 --> 00:15:06.168
- Yeah.

00:15:06.168 --> 00:15:07.555
I think, too...

00:15:07.555 --> 00:15:08.893
What are you doing, during your job?

00:15:08.893 --> 00:15:10.208
I think a lot people take a job

00:15:10.208 --> 00:15:12.138
that they're not learning anything.

00:15:12.138 --> 00:15:14.210
Or, if you want to, if you want to grow,

00:15:14.210 --> 00:15:15.043
like, can you take a job,

00:15:15.043 --> 00:15:16.097
where you're learning some of the skills

00:15:16.097 --> 00:15:18.428
you'll need to take into
your business as well.

00:15:18.428 --> 00:15:19.957
- [Daniel] Or, where you
have access to a computer,

00:15:19.957 --> 00:15:22.820
where you can kind of just
do your own thing sometimes.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:23.653
(Evan laughing)

00:15:23.653 --> 00:15:27.135
I mean, where we are, and
it's the truth, you know?

00:15:27.135 --> 00:15:27.968
- Alright.

00:15:27.968 --> 00:15:28.801
I like it.

00:15:28.801 --> 00:15:29.646
Listen, the security guard at my building

00:15:29.646 --> 00:15:31.132
likes to overnight.

00:15:31.132 --> 00:15:32.130
- [Daniel] Of course!

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:32.963
- Do his thing, so...

00:15:32.963 --> 00:15:34.620
- He better be workin' on Shopify store,

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:36.594
if you're a security guard!

00:15:36.594 --> 00:15:39.874
- [Evan] Alright, next up, Dion!

00:15:39.874 --> 00:15:41.891
"How can you deal with failure,

00:15:41.891 --> 00:15:45.571
"and still move forward into
what you want to become?"

00:15:45.571 --> 00:15:48.274
So, how do you deal with
failure, and still move forward?

00:15:48.274 --> 00:15:52.441
- [Daniel] Why is failure
seen as this poisonous event?

00:15:55.182 --> 00:15:56.435
The only problem with failure is that

00:15:56.435 --> 00:15:58.566
it's seen as very negative.

00:15:58.566 --> 00:15:59.776
But, what is the problem with that?

00:15:59.776 --> 00:16:00.609
I don't understand, like,

00:16:00.609 --> 00:16:02.406
let's change the narrative on failure.

00:16:02.406 --> 00:16:04.508
Let's not talking about
dealing with failure.

00:16:04.508 --> 00:16:07.605
Let's change our perspective,
on whether failure is bad,

00:16:07.605 --> 00:16:08.438
or not.

00:16:09.738 --> 00:16:12.825
There's a bunch of things
I'm failing at right now.

00:16:12.825 --> 00:16:14.088
You can have a book out,

00:16:14.088 --> 00:16:15.290
about how to start a successful business,

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:17.011
and still have trouble in business.

00:16:17.011 --> 00:16:18.793
You know, because every time you level up,

00:16:18.793 --> 00:16:20.303
you have to learn new stuff.

00:16:20.303 --> 00:16:21.995
So, you're going to fail all the time.

00:16:21.995 --> 00:16:23.896
The only way that you're
not going to fail,

00:16:23.896 --> 00:16:28.624
is if you don't do anything,
which is, in itself, a failure.

00:16:28.624 --> 00:16:29.707
There's no...

00:16:31.727 --> 00:16:33.172
We can go meta on you,

00:16:33.172 --> 00:16:36.322
and say that, like, the human
race is constantly failing.

00:16:36.322 --> 00:16:38.150
That's why, you know, we're
constantly trying to evolve,

00:16:38.150 --> 00:16:38.983
and change.

00:16:38.983 --> 00:16:40.684
I mean, so, there's
nothing wrong with failure.

00:16:40.684 --> 00:16:42.814
I think that you just need to learn that,

00:16:42.814 --> 00:16:45.151
that uncomfortable feeling that you get,

00:16:45.151 --> 00:16:46.225
when you fail at something,

00:16:46.225 --> 00:16:48.111
is just your ego, trying to protect you.

00:16:48.111 --> 00:16:49.702
But, you don't need protection.

00:16:49.702 --> 00:16:50.535
You're fine.

00:16:50.535 --> 00:16:52.242
Just keep going.

00:16:52.242 --> 00:16:54.212
- People are saying, "Love
that answer about failure.

00:16:54.212 --> 00:16:55.684
"Great answer about failure."

00:16:55.684 --> 00:16:57.803
Yeah, fail more!

00:16:57.803 --> 00:16:58.668
- But, you know what?

00:16:58.668 --> 00:17:01.372
But, let me just urge anyone
who's watching right now.

00:17:01.372 --> 00:17:04.516
It's also, be careful of
the trendy buzzword stuff,

00:17:04.516 --> 00:17:07.433
like hustle, authenticity, failure.

00:17:09.906 --> 00:17:11.688
These become buzzwords.

00:17:11.688 --> 00:17:13.637
But, I really want you
to take that to heart.

00:17:13.637 --> 00:17:15.795
Outside of the inspirational stuff,

00:17:15.795 --> 00:17:17.946
really take to heart what it means

00:17:17.946 --> 00:17:21.046
to be able to be resilient, and try again.

00:17:21.046 --> 00:17:23.925
There's nothing, there's
no personal attachment

00:17:23.925 --> 00:17:27.423
to you having some sort of,
struggling with something.

00:17:27.423 --> 00:17:28.990
You struggling with something

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:32.493
isn't an indemnification
on your personal being.

00:17:32.493 --> 00:17:35.390
You having some sort of challenge,

00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:36.992
that you're struggling to overcome,

00:17:36.992 --> 00:17:39.364
doesn't mean that you,
personally, are a failure.

00:17:39.364 --> 00:17:40.652
This is an event.

00:17:40.652 --> 00:17:42.542
So, I think that if we can all

00:17:42.542 --> 00:17:46.019
get a little bit less attached
to the results of things,

00:17:46.019 --> 00:17:47.429
it wouldn't be so painful.

00:17:47.429 --> 00:17:50.802
And then, we wouldn't be
so scared to try, you know?

00:17:50.802 --> 00:17:51.635
- [Evan] I like it.

00:17:51.635 --> 00:17:52.468
I like it.

00:17:52.468 --> 00:17:53.658
Daniel going meta on us.

00:17:53.658 --> 00:17:54.865
You can go meta anytime, man.

00:17:54.865 --> 00:17:56.157
I like it.

00:17:56.157 --> 00:17:57.147
Let's try and keep up with the chat!

00:17:57.147 --> 00:17:58.084
You guys are going nuts!

00:17:58.084 --> 00:17:59.257
I love it!

00:17:59.257 --> 00:18:01.045
Let's see, what else.

00:18:01.045 --> 00:18:02.133
Valley is asking,

00:18:02.133 --> 00:18:04.464
"How important, do you
think, networking is

00:18:04.464 --> 00:18:06.547
"for an online business?"

00:18:07.564 --> 00:18:11.386
- Well, there are two things,
that you need to know.

00:18:11.386 --> 00:18:13.195
Networking is, obviously, very important.

00:18:13.195 --> 00:18:17.465
But, the good thing is that
most people think that,

00:18:17.465 --> 00:18:18.526
it's kind of the same thing as,

00:18:18.526 --> 00:18:20.395
oh, you have to have money, to make money.

00:18:20.395 --> 00:18:21.228
Yes.

00:18:21.228 --> 00:18:23.214
But, there's a little asterisk here.

00:18:23.214 --> 00:18:25.364
So, it is really helpful, in business,

00:18:25.364 --> 00:18:27.204
to have already built up a network,

00:18:27.204 --> 00:18:28.566
because, then, they can help you.

00:18:28.566 --> 00:18:30.206
But, as you go into business,

00:18:30.206 --> 00:18:32.025
you're going to network
with people naturally,

00:18:32.025 --> 00:18:33.366
as long as you put yourself out there.

00:18:33.366 --> 00:18:36.135
So, I like to think of,
especially online business,

00:18:36.135 --> 00:18:37.378
I don't know about
other areas of business,

00:18:37.378 --> 00:18:38.959
but I think especially online...

00:18:38.959 --> 00:18:40.080
Have I ever told you this
analogy before, Evan?

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:43.080
It's like high school classes, okay?

00:18:44.007 --> 00:18:45.435
Depending on what year you start,

00:18:45.435 --> 00:18:46.808
everyone that starts with you,

00:18:46.808 --> 00:18:48.577
within a two, or three year period,

00:18:48.577 --> 00:18:50.778
you guys are in the
same high school class.

00:18:50.778 --> 00:18:52.482
And you guys help each other.

00:18:52.482 --> 00:18:55.828
Because the people who keep
going, rise up the ranks,

00:18:55.828 --> 00:18:59.648
and we just had our first book
pretty close to each other.

00:18:59.648 --> 00:19:00.806
So, we're in a similar class.

00:19:00.806 --> 00:19:02.356
I think you have more
followers on one platform,

00:19:02.356 --> 00:19:03.697
I have more followers on another platform,

00:19:03.697 --> 00:19:05.978
and a book out on similar times.

00:19:05.978 --> 00:19:08.492
We're kind of in the same
high school class here.

00:19:08.492 --> 00:19:10.042
Now, there are other people
who we probably look up to,

00:19:10.042 --> 00:19:12.413
who are like the juniors, and the seniors.

00:19:12.413 --> 00:19:14.780
I feel like I'm a sophomore, okay?

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:16.679
And they have more
followers, more traction,

00:19:16.679 --> 00:19:17.604
they've had more time in the game.

00:19:17.604 --> 00:19:19.268
But, we're networking
with each other right now.

00:19:19.268 --> 00:19:21.802
So, don't worry about
trying to always network

00:19:21.802 --> 00:19:23.662
with the big guys at the top,

00:19:23.662 --> 00:19:25.253
because you'll get there eventually.

00:19:25.253 --> 00:19:27.769
Network with people who
are your level right now,

00:19:27.769 --> 00:19:29.459
but who are doing things.

00:19:29.459 --> 00:19:30.686
And as you guys keep rising,

00:19:30.686 --> 00:19:32.498
you guys will see, that in five years,

00:19:32.498 --> 00:19:34.956
oh crap, you're both doing great work,

00:19:34.956 --> 00:19:35.911
and you've been spending all this time

00:19:35.911 --> 00:19:36.744
talking to each other.

00:19:36.744 --> 00:19:39.231
All my friends, who are
going to promote this book,

00:19:39.231 --> 00:19:41.028
I've known them since day one.

00:19:41.028 --> 00:19:43.509
They're not new friends.

00:19:43.509 --> 00:19:46.512
So, start now, but don't worry about

00:19:46.512 --> 00:19:48.110
always shooting for the top, first.

00:19:48.110 --> 00:19:49.869
Just do great work where you're at,

00:19:49.869 --> 00:19:53.079
and then, rise to the
top, with your peers.

00:19:53.079 --> 00:19:53.912
- [Evan] I like it.

00:19:53.912 --> 00:19:56.486
The high school class analogy.

00:19:56.486 --> 00:19:57.470
So, I like that.

00:19:57.470 --> 00:19:58.303
- I'm not a freshman, though.

00:19:58.303 --> 00:19:59.716
I am not a freshman anymore.

00:19:59.716 --> 00:20:00.607
(Evan laughing)

00:20:00.607 --> 00:20:01.986
I'm out of that.

00:20:01.986 --> 00:20:04.386
- [Evan] Okay, Eric wants
to go super specific.

00:20:04.386 --> 00:20:07.176
Let's see what we got here.

00:20:07.176 --> 00:20:11.715
"Daniel, can you please talk
about how to start a tea shop?

00:20:11.715 --> 00:20:13.207
"Any advice would be awesome."

00:20:13.207 --> 00:20:14.348
- [Daniel] Okay.

00:20:14.348 --> 00:20:17.168
So, oh, we're talkin' like...

00:20:17.168 --> 00:20:18.938
Like, consumable tea, right, like...

00:20:18.938 --> 00:20:20.851
- [Evan] I'm assuming, I'm assuming.

00:20:20.851 --> 00:20:22.401
- Okay, so, this is a good one.

00:20:22.401 --> 00:20:24.520
There are a couple
different ways to do it.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:26.229
So, the first thing I think of,

00:20:26.229 --> 00:20:28.142
is you have to have a unique angle,

00:20:28.142 --> 00:20:29.199
because that market is,

00:20:29.199 --> 00:20:30.627
I mean, all the markets
are kind of flooded.

00:20:30.627 --> 00:20:31.941
So, what's your unique angle?

00:20:31.941 --> 00:20:34.278
I have one of my students...

00:20:34.278 --> 00:20:38.809
Who has a tea company
called Jordan and Jane's,

00:20:38.809 --> 00:20:41.862
and her name is Jordan, and
Jane refers to Mary Jane,

00:20:41.862 --> 00:20:44.202
which is like cannabis-infused tea.

00:20:44.202 --> 00:20:45.842
But, it's not, I don't
think it's psychoactive,

00:20:45.842 --> 00:20:46.924
'cause she's in Colorado.

00:20:46.924 --> 00:20:47.833
But, I don't think it's psychoactive.

00:20:47.833 --> 00:20:50.204
I think it's more of just like...

00:20:50.204 --> 00:20:51.795
Like, hemp tea, right?

00:20:51.795 --> 00:20:53.284
But, that is a unique angle,

00:20:53.284 --> 00:20:54.874
because there's a lot of
different health properties

00:20:54.874 --> 00:20:56.504
associated with hemp and cannabis.

00:20:56.504 --> 00:20:58.496
So, she has a unique angle,

00:20:58.496 --> 00:21:00.978
where she adds very nice flavors to it.

00:21:00.978 --> 00:21:02.589
Plus, there's the cannabis angle.

00:21:02.589 --> 00:21:03.619
So, I would try to find an angle.

00:21:03.619 --> 00:21:05.828
If you look online now, on Instagram,

00:21:05.828 --> 00:21:07.018
a lot of these fitness models

00:21:07.018 --> 00:21:08.571
are doing this skinny tea thing,

00:21:08.571 --> 00:21:10.638
where this tea, this detox tea cleanse.

00:21:10.638 --> 00:21:11.471
Are you in that world, Evan?

00:21:11.471 --> 00:21:12.893
Do you know about that?

00:21:12.893 --> 00:21:13.726
- [Evan] No.

00:21:13.726 --> 00:21:15.493
- [Daniel] Okay, this is
a whole world of girls

00:21:15.493 --> 00:21:18.132
who want to like get
rid of the tummy bloat.

00:21:18.132 --> 00:21:19.948
So, it's called Skinny Tummy Tea,

00:21:19.948 --> 00:21:21.661
and they have it in the shaker,

00:21:21.661 --> 00:21:24.322
and I don't know if it
works, I haven't tried it.

00:21:24.322 --> 00:21:26.012
But, apparently, you drink it,

00:21:26.012 --> 00:21:27.594
and it just gets rid of
the digestive issues,

00:21:27.594 --> 00:21:28.572
and the bloats.

00:21:28.572 --> 00:21:30.563
So, that's another type of tea,

00:21:30.563 --> 00:21:32.603
where you have to have a
specific purpose for it.

00:21:32.603 --> 00:21:34.224
If you're just going to make green tea,

00:21:34.224 --> 00:21:35.810
you know, I think Earl's got you covered.

00:21:35.810 --> 00:21:36.980
Like, Earl Grey tea is good.

00:21:36.980 --> 00:21:38.910
Like, Lipton has got that market.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:42.817
So, try to go for something
specific in niche,

00:21:42.817 --> 00:21:43.986
so that you can stand out in a way,

00:21:43.986 --> 00:21:46.956
where even if you only capture
one percent of the market,

00:21:46.956 --> 00:21:48.334
or half the percent, or a quarter percent,

00:21:48.334 --> 00:21:51.603
they're really enthusiastic
quarter percent.

00:21:51.603 --> 00:21:52.436
- [Evan] Alright.

00:21:52.436 --> 00:21:53.269
I like it.

00:21:53.269 --> 00:21:54.102
Super tactical.

00:21:54.102 --> 00:21:55.234
- [Daniel] Bam, bam, bam, bam, bam!

00:21:55.234 --> 00:21:56.067
- [Evan] Next up!

00:21:56.067 --> 00:21:57.320
"Hey, Daniel!"

00:21:57.320 --> 00:21:58.733
- [Daniel] Hey!

00:21:58.733 --> 00:22:02.571
- "Why are startups not
usually successful?"

00:22:02.571 --> 00:22:04.571
- [Daniel] Is that true?

00:22:06.231 --> 00:22:07.974
Is that true?

00:22:07.974 --> 00:22:09.483
You know what?

00:22:09.483 --> 00:22:10.369
I would challenge--

00:22:10.369 --> 00:22:11.202
- [Evan] We're going to go meta.

00:22:11.202 --> 00:22:12.035
We're going to go meta, again.

00:22:12.035 --> 00:22:13.072
Here it comes!

00:22:13.072 --> 00:22:14.213
- Okay.

00:22:14.213 --> 00:22:15.562
But, here's a new train of thought

00:22:15.562 --> 00:22:16.644
that I've been playing with.

00:22:16.644 --> 00:22:19.065
And so, I'm going to debut
this on your show, okay Evan?

00:22:19.065 --> 00:22:19.898
- [Evan] Alright.

00:22:19.898 --> 00:22:20.731
I like it.

00:22:20.731 --> 00:22:21.564
- [Daniel] This is a new,

00:22:21.564 --> 00:22:24.418
a new piece of thought
leadership I've been developing.

00:22:24.418 --> 00:22:26.803
We need to start asking better questions,

00:22:26.803 --> 00:22:29.490
because the questions that we ask

00:22:29.490 --> 00:22:31.658
create the answers that we receive.

00:22:31.658 --> 00:22:33.310
And so, when you ask a question like

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:35.020
why aren't startups successful,

00:22:35.020 --> 00:22:37.289
you're going to get a lot of
reasons why they're not successful,

00:22:37.289 --> 00:22:39.092
and you're going to naturally
return these things.

00:22:39.092 --> 00:22:43.340
Maxwell Maltz wrote a book
called Psycho-Cybernetics,

00:22:43.340 --> 00:22:44.847
where he talks about using

00:22:44.847 --> 00:22:46.365
the reticular activation system.

00:22:46.365 --> 00:22:47.744
And I don't know if this is pseudoscience,

00:22:47.744 --> 00:22:49.924
but I just remember the terminology.

00:22:49.924 --> 00:22:50.975
Basically, the idea is,

00:22:50.975 --> 00:22:52.525
if you think of a problem backwards,

00:22:52.525 --> 00:22:53.825
you can find the solution.

00:22:53.825 --> 00:22:54.716
Well, if you think about the problem,

00:22:54.716 --> 00:22:56.353
like why don't things work,

00:22:56.353 --> 00:22:57.885
you're going to think of all
the reasons why it doesn't work.

00:22:57.885 --> 00:23:01.127
So, I would challenge you,
rather than focusing on that,

00:23:01.127 --> 00:23:02.093
and I'm not blowing off the answer,

00:23:02.093 --> 00:23:05.766
but I would say, think
about why startups do work,

00:23:05.766 --> 00:23:07.974
and focus on that, and just do that.

00:23:07.974 --> 00:23:09.344
The main reason why they don't work,

00:23:09.344 --> 00:23:11.013
is for a few reasons.

00:23:11.013 --> 00:23:15.180
One, because the founders
just don't want to keep going.

00:23:16.061 --> 00:23:17.193
It's really hard.

00:23:17.193 --> 00:23:18.183
I think that's the number one reason.

00:23:18.183 --> 00:23:20.153
But also, obviously, there
are also market issues,

00:23:20.153 --> 00:23:22.023
market fit, money.

00:23:22.023 --> 00:23:24.661
You know, people can get
screwed over by a partner.

00:23:24.661 --> 00:23:26.193
All these things can happen,

00:23:26.193 --> 00:23:27.990
but at the end of the
day, it's really hard,

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:30.036
just business is very hard.

00:23:30.036 --> 00:23:31.990
And if you get lucky in the beginning,

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:34.393
you're going to need to build
skill to backup that luck.

00:23:34.393 --> 00:23:35.682
If you have skill in the beginning,

00:23:35.682 --> 00:23:37.922
you're still going to need some
luck, to get you to the top.

00:23:37.922 --> 00:23:39.252
You need skill and luck.

00:23:39.252 --> 00:23:42.781
And the only way you get
skill and luck, is with time.

00:23:42.781 --> 00:23:44.421
So, if you don't put in the time,

00:23:44.421 --> 00:23:46.212
you won't get those two
attributes, skill and luck,

00:23:46.212 --> 00:23:47.733
and you're just going to fall apart.

00:23:47.733 --> 00:23:50.833
So, rather than think about
why it's not going to work,

00:23:50.833 --> 00:23:53.782
find the reasons why businesses
do work, and emulate that.

00:23:53.782 --> 00:23:54.948
Don't run from what you don't want.

00:23:54.948 --> 00:23:57.619
Run towards what you do want.

00:23:57.619 --> 00:23:58.452
- [Evan] I like it!

00:23:58.452 --> 00:23:59.285
The debut!

00:23:59.285 --> 00:24:00.620
- [Daniel] Boom, boom, boom, boom!

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:02.251
- You should go heavy on that stuff, man.

00:24:02.251 --> 00:24:03.084
I like it.

00:24:03.084 --> 00:24:04.628
- [Daniel] Boom.

00:24:04.628 --> 00:24:06.545
- SanFranMan is saying,

00:24:07.728 --> 00:24:09.536
"Daniel, love your website!"

00:24:09.536 --> 00:24:13.939
You guys can check that
out, Rich20Something.com.

00:24:13.939 --> 00:24:16.519
"I have a lot of different
potential business ideas.

00:24:16.519 --> 00:24:18.470
"Can you talk about focusing on,

00:24:18.470 --> 00:24:21.001
"and choosing a single
idea to go all in on,

00:24:21.001 --> 00:24:22.540
"and start your business?"

00:24:22.540 --> 00:24:23.648
- [Daniel] Yeah, this is a good one.

00:24:23.648 --> 00:24:25.038
And you know what's funny, Evan?

00:24:25.038 --> 00:24:26.309
I'll tell you this.

00:24:26.309 --> 00:24:28.469
Having too many ideas
is almost more painful

00:24:28.469 --> 00:24:30.895
than having no ideas, right?

00:24:30.895 --> 00:24:33.394
Because you feel like,
man, all of these could be,

00:24:33.394 --> 00:24:34.465
you're watching Shark Tank,

00:24:34.465 --> 00:24:36.343
and you're like, man, all three of,

00:24:36.343 --> 00:24:38.703
three of those five inventions
I've already had in my head,

00:24:38.703 --> 00:24:41.703
and I haven't done anything with it.

00:24:42.914 --> 00:24:45.248
Picking one idea, out of the three,

00:24:45.248 --> 00:24:47.904
let's say you have three ideas...

00:24:47.904 --> 00:24:50.284
I think we all kind of,
in the back of our heads,

00:24:50.284 --> 00:24:52.365
know which idea is usually the best.

00:24:52.365 --> 00:24:53.787
I think we all kind of know.

00:24:53.787 --> 00:24:55.880
I think that when we have multiple ideas,

00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:57.589
some of the competing ideas that we have

00:24:57.589 --> 00:25:00.007
are things that are like
our passion projects,

00:25:00.007 --> 00:25:01.479
and our fantasies.

00:25:01.479 --> 00:25:03.870
And some of the ones, that we
know are going to be the ones,

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:05.258
are going to be the most successful.

00:25:05.258 --> 00:25:08.627
I would say, pick the one,
as the lowest hanging fruit

00:25:08.627 --> 00:25:09.478
in the beginning,

00:25:09.478 --> 00:25:10.958
with the lowest startup capital,

00:25:10.958 --> 00:25:13.347
and the lowest amount of inherent risk,

00:25:13.347 --> 00:25:14.717
which is why I always like a service,

00:25:14.717 --> 00:25:16.757
'cause if you don't like the service,

00:25:16.757 --> 00:25:17.590
you can always quit it.

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:18.423
There's no inventory.

00:25:18.423 --> 00:25:19.538
There's no systems to shut down.

00:25:19.538 --> 00:25:20.978
It's just you.

00:25:20.978 --> 00:25:22.292
And then, also remember,

00:25:22.292 --> 00:25:23.709
that whatever you do now,

00:25:23.709 --> 00:25:25.778
doesn't have to be the thing
that you're doing next year,

00:25:25.778 --> 00:25:27.119
or in three, or five years.

00:25:27.119 --> 00:25:28.672
You can always start something now.

00:25:28.672 --> 00:25:30.622
And then, you can still
keep those other ideas,

00:25:30.622 --> 00:25:31.653
and do 'em later.

00:25:31.653 --> 00:25:34.233
There's no statue of
limitations, or time limit,

00:25:34.233 --> 00:25:35.922
on when you can do an idea.

00:25:35.922 --> 00:25:37.281
Obviously, there's the market changes,

00:25:37.281 --> 00:25:38.599
and people move in, and move out.

00:25:38.599 --> 00:25:40.079
But, those ideas are yours.

00:25:40.079 --> 00:25:41.449
Like, I still want to learn
how to play the piano,

00:25:41.449 --> 00:25:43.167
and I just don't have time right now.

00:25:43.167 --> 00:25:44.218
But, it's on my list.

00:25:44.218 --> 00:25:46.049
And I will learn, before I die.

00:25:46.049 --> 00:25:47.370
But, I'm not, you know,

00:25:47.370 --> 00:25:50.029
(stammers) I might not start
for the next ten years.

00:25:50.029 --> 00:25:50.862
But, that's fine.

00:25:50.862 --> 00:25:52.760
So, just keep those ideas
in your back pocket.

00:25:52.760 --> 00:25:54.919
Work on one that seems to
be the easiest to get to,

00:25:54.919 --> 00:25:55.752
right now.

00:25:55.752 --> 00:25:57.938
And then, keep going.

00:25:57.938 --> 00:25:58.771
- [Evan] I like it.

00:25:58.771 --> 00:25:59.790
Dion is saying,

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:01.998
"The more that I'm listening to this,

00:26:01.998 --> 00:26:03.789
"the more I'm being
confident that I'll make it.

00:26:03.789 --> 00:26:05.217
"Thanks, Evan and Daniel."

00:26:05.217 --> 00:26:08.070
Oh, "Thanks Evan, and big ups, to Daniel.

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:10.855
"You should run for office someday."

00:26:10.855 --> 00:26:12.204
- Oh!

00:26:12.204 --> 00:26:13.037
Okay.

00:26:13.037 --> 00:26:14.079
This is also the first time I've ever,

00:26:14.079 --> 00:26:15.980
you're gettin' a lot of exclusives.

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:17.647
I have a goal, by...

00:26:19.210 --> 00:26:20.543
By, by 20 years.

